## STILL MISSING

### No CARDINALITY, so |G| = [G:H]·|H| is a statement about LISTINGS

Judson's Lagrange is an equation between numbers: `|G|/|H| = [G:H]`. 2b4m has no
cardinality function on a sort, so the whole chapter is phrased with LISTINGS — a
sequence whose entries below a length are pairwise distinct and exhaust what they
list (the idiom from `std/permutation/count.b4m`). `std/group/lagrange.b4m` therefore
proves:

> k coset representatives + a p-long listing of H  ⟹  a (k·p)-long listing of G.

That is the mathematical content, and it is what the corollaries actually consume. But
it is NOT Judson's sentence, and three things are awkward as a result:

- **"the number of distinct left cosets"** has no term. `[G:H]` enters as a HYPOTHESIS
  (you are handed k representatives) rather than being DEFINED from the group and the
  subgroup. Nothing stops a caller supplying a k that is wrong; the theorem is still
  true, it just says less than Judson's. (This is the one genuinely weaker part. The
  SUBGROUP's fixedness is NOT a limitation — see "Lagrange generalizes" below.)
- **Divisibility needs a bridge.** `|H| divides |G|` is not a restatement of the
  listing theorem — it needs "G is listed by n" + "G is listed by k·p" ⟹ n = k·p
  (listing-length uniqueness) and then `p | n` from the product. The uniqueness half
  is the load-bearing piece and is not currently stated for group listings.
- **Corollary chains multiply.** `[G:K] = [G:H][H:K]` is division arithmetic in
  Judson; as listings it is a three-way block decomposition, i.e. a second instance of
  the whole Lagrange construction rather than two divisions.

Not a defect — the listing idiom is a deliberate choice and it carries the content.
But it means each §6.2 corollary is its own piece of work, not a one-liner off the
theorem, and the chapter should say so rather than imply the corollaries are free.

### A listing's length is not known to be unique (for groups)

Needed by every divisibility corollary. For PERMUTATIONS this exists —
`std/permutation/count.b4m` has the pigeonhole machinery and
`listingsInBijectionHaveEqualLengths` (~472 lines) — but it hardcodes `compose` and
the permutation sort. Generalizing it needs either sort polymorphism (absent) or a
re-proof over the group sort. See the next entry: this is the same wall.

### `Seq` is re-postulated per carrier — five copies and counting

`std/sequence.b4m` has the abstract fold, but each concrete sequence theory must
RESTATE the sequence-EXISTENCE postulates over its own carrier, because a model
transfers THEOREMS and must DISCHARGE axioms — and `seqSingleton`/`seqConcat`/… are
axioms. So `std/group/sequence.b4m`, `std/integer/sequence.b4m`,
`std/permutation/sequence.b4m`, `std/integer/mod-n-listing.b4m` and
`std/set/subsets.b4m` each carry their own copy, each with a comment explaining why.

This is what FIXED THE BASE SORT for Chapter 6. `std/group/sequence.b4m` postulates
`Seq`/`at` over `std/group.b4m`'s `Grp`, so anything that counts group elements must
live over THAT sort — the listing layer chose the carrier, and `std/group/coset.b4m`
had to follow it (not the other way round). Recorded in memory as
`abstract-collections-direction`: the fix is a container theory parameterized by its
carrier, so a sequence over any sort is one model away instead of five re-postulated
axiom sets. Until then, every new counting theory pays the copy.

### Two same-named sorts from different origins print identically

`expected sort 'Grp', got 'Grp'` — the diagnostic names NEITHER origin. Hit while
importing `std/subgroup.b4m` (which then declared its own `Grp`) as a base for
cosets. It cost a full detour: I read the message as an obstacle to route around,
concluded the FILE was wrong, and wrote a commit message blaming it, when the message
was really saying "these are two different sorts that happen to share a name".

Less likely to fire in std now that `subgroup` aliases (`1b8b87a`), but the
diagnostic is unchanged and will catch the next person. Printing the origin file for
each side is a cheap fix.

### `--axioms` lists an axiom per ORIGIN, and that reads like a bug

When `std/group/coset.b4m` declared its own copies of the three subgroup criteria and
reached `std/subgroup.b4m` through a model, `--axioms` listed each criterion TWICE —
once per site. I wrote this off as "the ledger double-reporting a model transfer,"
compared it against `tests/cases/model_subgroup_transfer.b4m` (which shows the same
shape, for the same reason), and moved on.

It was not double-reporting. It was correctly reporting TWO ORIGINS — which is exactly
the duplication I should have removed. Aliasing collapsed it to one entry each.

**Lesson for the next reader of this file:** a repeated axiom NAME at two sites in
`--axioms` means two declarations, never one fact counted twice. The ledger is right;
check the design.

### A FIXED-CONSTANT development is reachable ONLY through a model nobody wrote

`std/group/order.b4m` has a large section built on fixed constants:

```2b4m
const A: Grp
const N: Int
axiom orderPositive: less_than(ZERO, N)
axiom orderKills: power(A, N) = E
axiom orderMinimal: forall m: Int; less_than(ZERO, m) -> less_than(m, N) -> power(A, m) != E
```

Everything proved there — `killedExponentIsMultiple`, `powerEqIdentityIffOrderDivides`,
`powerCofactorKills` — is a theorem about THOSE CONSTANTS, and **nothing in std, aata or
tests cites any of them** (checked by grep on each name). So the section reads as
unreachable, and that is how I first recorded it here.

**That was wrong, and the correction is the useful part.** A model DOES reach it. Mapping
`order.A` and `order.N` onto a local element and its order, and discharging the three order
axioms from a `hasOrder` hypothesis, transfers the whole corpus — verified end to end in
`tests/cases/model_element_order.b4m`, where `everyPowerReducesBelowTheOrder` lands at a
variable element and `--axioms` confirms it rests on the local order hypothesis plus the
group/power/integer axioms, nothing else. The section is not sealed; it is simply
**undiscovered**, with no fixture showing the entry point.

The cost of the gap was real anyway: I added `everyPowerReducesBelowTheOrder` to that
section, then — believing it unreachable — proved the SAME argument again over a variable
`g` as `counting.everyPowerIsOneOfTheFirstN`. Two copies of one division-algorithm
argument. And the duplication predates this chapter:
`counting.smallerIndexGivesADifferentPower` also re-derives from the `hasOrder` definition
rather than transferring.

**Lesson, and it generalizes past this file:** a fixed-constant + axioms section is a
MODEL SOURCE, and it is indistinguishable from dead code unless something demonstrates the
model. `std/` already has the convention that a library demonstrates its own axioms
(`<namespace>/examples.b4m`, enforced by `--library`); the same argument applies to a
model-source section, and `--library` does NOT catch it, because it flags unused AXIOMS,
not an unused development. A fixed-constant section wants its example.

### std has no INDEX, so a differently-named twin is invisible

I wrote 90 lines of a `powerSequenceExists` for Corollary 6.7 before discovering
`counting.powersAreListed` is exactly that theorem, already proved. Grep on the name
I had chosen found nothing, because the existing one is named differently; what would
have found it is a search by STATEMENT SHAPE ("exists v: Seq; … at(v, i) = power(…)"),
which nothing supports.

1086 theorems across 91 files is past the size where a reader can hold the inventory.
A per-theory "what this file proves" index (one line per takeaway result) would catch
this; the std-file-layout convention already asks each parent to forward its
sub-theories' takeaway results, which is half the mechanism. `2b4m query` could
plausibly do the other half (search declarations by statement shape).

### RESOLVED — `Points` was missing a CONCAT postulate (the thinnest-carrier case)

Left here because the diagnosis is the reusable part. §6.2's cycle-length theorem needs
the IMAGE POINT-LIST — a `Points` whose i-th entry is σ(sᵢ) — and that could not be
built. `Points` had only TWO building postulates (`pointsSingleton`, `pointsPrepend`)
where `std/group/sequence.b4m`'s `Seq` has five.

Both natural proof shapes were unavailable, and the second is the non-obvious one:

- Fix the list and grow the image at the top (what `translatedSequenceExists` does):
  needs an append. Not available.
- Generalize the list INSIDE the induction and prepend σ(s₀) before an image list for the
  TAIL: needs a list's tail, and `pointsPrepend` only adds at the FRONT (shifting entries
  UP), so from an arbitrary s there is no way to reach a list whose i-th entry is s_{i+1}.
  Verified by stating it — well-formed, but only as a `hole`.

**Fixed by adding `pointsConcat`** (user decision, 2026-10-02), in the same shape as
`seqConcat` minus the product clause since `Points` carries no fold. The theorem then
followed: `imagePointsExist` by the ordinary prefix-growing induction, and
`conjugateOfACycle` by induction on the length with `conjugationIsMultiplicative`
splitting σ(T∘C)σ⁻¹ into (σTσ⁻¹)(σCσ⁻¹).

**The lesson that outlives the fix:** a carrier's postulate set silently determines which
INDUCTION SHAPES are available to it, and the failure does not look like a missing axiom —
it looks like two unrelated proof attempts not working. A carrier-parameterized container
theory (memory: `abstract-collections-direction`) would have given `Points` all five
postulates for free; until then, when a construction over a fresh sequence sort will not
go, compare its postulate list against `std/group/sequence.b4m`'s before assuming the
proof is wrong.

### §6.3 (Fermat/Euler): the schema gap is FIXED; what remains is that a model is FILE-LOCAL

Two rounds of diagnosis here, both recorded because the first was wrong.

**Round 1 (wrong).** I wrote that §6.3 is blocked because "a schema cannot be instantiated
at a predicate of your choosing through a model". A repro showed the parse error was real
but the generalization was not.

**Round 2 — FIXED in the engine** (`f8eef96`, user-directed). Two gaps, now closed:
- `[using model(M) src.sch(myPred)]` parses. A model-cited schema takes its instantiation
  arguments after the cited name, the same slot `instantiation NAME(args)` uses.
- A PROVEN source schema may be discharged with `<-`. Previously only an AXIOM schema could
  be; a proven one was refused as "a theorem — it materializes through the mapped axioms",
  which is true of a plain theorem and false of a schema (an instantiation is per-instance
  proof, so there is nothing to materialize from).
- And the bare form outside a schema body is now an error, naming both fixes.

Trust boundary verified: a citer gets the DISCHARGING schema's statement instantiated, not
the source's. A weak-but-proven discharge standing in for a strong source yields only the
weak fact (the claim is matched against the instance), and `--axioms` names the discharge.

**WHAT STILL BLOCKS §6.3: a `model` is FILE-LOCAL.** `[using model(mod_n_listing.ZnListing)
group_listing.splittingAListing(isUnit)]` fails with "unknown model
'mod_n_listing.ZnListing'" — a model declares no name a *consumer* can reach, by design
(memory: `import-vs-model-axes`). Verified: no model is cited across a file boundary
anywhere in the tree.

So reaching `splittingAListing` at ℤ_n's `isUnit` means doing it INSIDE
`std/integer/mod-n-listing.b4m`, which already has `ZnListing` — and that file must then
declare its own local stand-in schema for the model to discharge. That is still a stand-in
per carrier, but with the engine fix it is a STATEMENT to write, not a PROOF to redo: the
local schema's body can be the one-line model cite, and `permutation/listing.b4m`'s
full re-proof of `splittingAListing` (line 1462) is no longer the only route.

Remaining for §6.3, in order: (1) the local stand-in + the split at `isUnit` inside
mod-n-listing, giving U(n)'s listing and hence φ(n); (2) Corollary 6.7 applied in U(n) for
Euler; (3) φ(p) = p−1 for the Fermat specialization. None started.

### A LISTING LENGTH is a ℕ but PRIMALITY is a ℤ notion, and no bridge existed

Judson's Corollary 6.8 needs "the order divides |G|, and |G| is prime, so the order is
1 or |G|". The order and the group's size are both ℕ — they are LISTING LENGTHS — while
`std/primes.b4m` states `is_prime` over ℤ, and `std/peano/divides.b4m` has no primality
notion at all. Neither direction existed:

- no `toIntMul` (the embedding had `toIntAdd` but not its multiplicative twin), and
- no transport of `divides` across `toInt`.

Both landed (`70a99d2`) and are reusable well beyond this corollary, but they were a
prerequisite nobody had hit, which is itself the signal: ℕ and ℤ have parallel
`divides`/`gcd`/order theories and the embedding carries very little between them. The
next cross-sort arithmetic fact will hit the same wall. (Judson has no such seam — he
works in ℤ throughout and |G| is just a number.)

### `or` is LEFT-associative, so a 3-way `case` is two nested splits

`a or b or c` parses as `(a or b) or c`, so `case` on a trichotomy cannot take three
arms — the first arm must assume `a or b` and split again. The diagnostic is good
("or_elim: subproof must assume 'less_than(i, j) or i = j'") and names exactly the arm
it wanted, which is the tell. Worth knowing before writing the arms, since restructuring
afterwards is a re-indent of everything.

### A definition-block clause is UNIVERSALLY QUANTIFIED

`pred inCyclic(g: Grp): inCyclic(g) iff inGenerated(gen, g)` makes
`[by definition inCyclic]` yield `forall g; inCyclic(g) iff …`, not the instance at the
term you want. Every use needs its own `specialize` — eight sites in
`std/group/order-divides.b4m`. Reasonable once known (it IS an axiom about all `g`), but
the obvious reading of "cite the definition" is the instance.

### A theorem's TERM arguments and HYPOTHESES are separate lists, and the error is oblique

`counting.fullOrderGenerates` interleaves them in its statement
(`forall n, w; <two hypotheses> -> forall g; hasOrder(g, …) -> …`), so `g` is a term
argument that comes AFTER two hypotheses. Writing
`specialize fullOrderGenerates(n, gl) hyp1 hyp2 gen hyp3` — hypotheses and the term in
citation order — fails with "'gen' names an identifier, not an axiom/theorem". The right
form puts every term in the parens regardless of where its binder sits:
`fullOrderGenerates(n, gl, gen) hyp1 hyp2 hyp3`. The message is accurate but describes
the symptom, not the rule.

### `2b4m fmt` CRASHES on an unbalanced proof brace

```
thread N panic: integer overflow
src/fmt.zig:191:43 in render     if (in_proof) extra_depth -= 1;
```

`extra_depth` is unsigned and decremented past zero. One too many `}` in a proof
core-dumps the formatter instead of diagnosing, which violates the repo's
hard-errors-not-warnings rule and tells the author nothing about WHERE. `check`
diagnoses the same file fine, so the workaround is to run `check` first; the useful
locator in the meantime is a one-liner printing running brace depth at each `qed`
(a nonzero value names the offending theorem).

### `case` is a step's JUSTIFICATION, not a step of its own

Minor, but it cost two cycles. `case <disj> { … }` goes in the justification slot of
the step that states the case-split's CONCLUSION:

```2b4m
@conclusion-indices-equal |
  i = j
  case i-splits {
    @when-i-is-low | assume … { … }
    @when-i-is-high | assume … { … }
  }
```

Writing it as a bare `@by-case | case i-splits { … }` step is a parse error
("expected an expression, got 'case'"). The cheatsheet shows the form inline but not
that the conclusion formula must be on the step carrying it.

### The index-arithmetic lemmas live in the wrong file

`indexSplits`, `belowTheBaseIsBelowTheSum` and `indexPastTheBase` are pure
Peano-order facts — "an index below base+m is either below base or is base+j for some
j<m" — with NO permutation content, but they are declared in
`std/permutation/count.b4m`. Three unrelated files now alias them out of there:
`std/permutation/dihedral.b4m`, `std/integer/mod-n-generators.b4m`, and now
`std/group/lagrange.b4m`. Any file doing a block decomposition needs them, and none
of those should import the permutation counting theory to get them. They belong in
`std/peano/order.b4m` (or a `std/peano/block-index.b4m`). Mechanical move.

